and I've linked to some of my faves in the notes attached to this video.

61
00:04:36,878 --> 00:04:42,288
在我看来，学习任何缩写词最好的方法就是关注每一个字母。
In my opinion, the best way to start learning about any acronym is to focus on each individual letter.

62
00:04:42,510 --> 00:04:46,948
对于 API，我觉得我们应该从最后一个字母 I，也就是 interface（接口）开始。
For A, P, I, I think we should start on that last character, the I, interface.

63
00:04:47,248 --> 00:04:50,180
让我们来聊聊你们已经见过的那些接口。
Let's talk a bit about the interfaces that you've seen already.

64
00:04:51,342 --> 00:04:53,060
接口就在我们身边。
Interfaces are all around us.

65
00:04:53,328 --> 00:04:56,768
例如，这个收音机，它有一个很熟悉的接口。
For instance, this radio here, it has a familiar interface.

66
00:04:56,928 --> 00:05:02,500
这个接口让我可以调节音量、切换频道，还可以开关机。
The interface allows me to do things like change the volume, change the station, I can turn it off and on.

67
00:05:02,848 --> 00:05:06,448
我完全不知道这机器内部是怎么运作的。
I literally have no idea how anything inside this thing works.

68
00:05:06,670 --> 00:05:11,108
但即便如此，接口仍让我能通过暴露出来的选项来控制它的运作方式。
But still, the interface allows me to control the way that it works through the options that are exposed.

69
00:05:11,726 --> 00:05:12,996
这真的很棒，不是吗？
And that's pretty great, isn't it?

70
00:05:13,262 --> 00:05:16,368
正在发生的那些神奇过程，对我来说完全是抽象掉的。
The magic that is happening is completely abstracted away from me.

71
00:05:16,448 --> 00:05:20,420
我依然掌控着作为用户所能处理的那些确定项。
I'm still in control of what has been determined that I, a user, can handle.

72
00:05:21,102 --> 00:05:23,328
接口通常会随着时间的推移而演变。
Interfaces usually evolve over time.

73
00:05:23,630 --> 00:05:25,648
比如闹钟收音机。
Like an alarm clock radio, for example.

74
00:05:25,808 --> 00:05:32,768
它保留了我熟悉的音量和频道控制，同时也增加了一些新功能，比如更改时间和设置闹钟。
It has my familiar volume and station controls, and it also adds some new features, like changing the time and setting the alarm.

75
00:05:32,928 --> 00:05:38,928
我仍然不知道它的具体实现是怎么运作的，但接口是我能理解的东西。
I still have no idea how this works implementation-wise, but the interface is something that I can understand.

76
00:05:39,728 --> 00:05:40,516
通常情况下是这样。
Usually.

77
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:52,804
随着技术的进步和新选项的出现，它们会进入新版本的接口中，
依然为我们抽象掉复杂细节，但让我们能够掌控这些新功能。
And as technology evolves and new options come into existence, they make their way into the new versions of the interface,
still abstracting away things for us, but enabling us to be in control of the new features.

78
00:05:53,262 --> 00:05:58,052
有时这些物理接口变得非常深入人心，甚至变成了 GUI。
Sometimes these physical interfaces get so recognizable they even make it into GUIs.

79
00:05:58,670 --> 00:05:59,408
嘿，你听到了吗？
Hey, did you hear that?

80
00:05:59,598 --> 00:06:00,208
又来了。
There it is again.

81
00:06:00,430 --> 00:06:03,940
GUI，G-U-I，图形用户界面。
GUI, G-U-I, graphical user interface.

82
00:06:04,558 --> 00:06:06,768
这是我手机上的流媒体音乐应用。
This is my streaming music app on my phone.

83
00:06:07,022 --> 00:06:10,288
在用户界面，也就是 UI 里，有我熟悉的播放按钮。
There's my familiar play button in the user interface, or UI.

84
00:06:11,008 --> 00:06:16,612
我知道按下播放按钮会发生什么，但我不知道它是怎么实现的。
And I know what the play button does when I press it, but I have no idea how it does it.

85
00:06:16,782 --> 00:06:18,848
我接受这种程度的抽象。
And I'm okay with that level of abstraction.

86
00:06:19,088 --> 00:06:21,088
如果没有音乐，我在公共交通工具上会做什么？
What would I do in public transit without music?

87
00:06:21,248 --> 00:06:22,448
我会和人聊天。
I talk to people.

88
00:06:22,688 --> 00:06:24,288
嗨，最近怎么样？
Hi, how's it going?

89
00:06:26,528 --> 00:06:31,968
现在，编写这个流媒体音乐应用的开发者当然了解播放按钮是如何工作的。
Now, the developer who wrote this streaming music application, of course, understands how the play button works.

90
00:06:32,128 --> 00:06:35,716
她编写了按钮的代码，这样当它被点击时，音乐就会开始播放。
She coded the button so that when it gets clicked, the music starts playing.

91
00:06:36,398 --> 00:06:42,948
不过问题是，我们的开发者也在使用一些接口，这些接口帮她屏蔽了一些细节。
Here's the thing though, our developer is also working with quite a
few interfaces that are abstracting away some of the details for her.

92
00:06:43,310 --> 00:06:46,532
例如，她编写了按钮被点击时触发的代码。
For instance, she wrote code that happens when the button is clicked.

93
00:06:46,958 --> 00:06:50,660
她可能是通过按钮上的事件处理器，比如点击事件，来实现这一点的。
She probably did that using an event handler on the button, like on click.

94
00:06:51,406 --> 00:06:56,516
然而，她不需要编写让按钮产生交互并随按压而改变的代码。
What she didn't have to do, however, was write the code that makes the button interact and change with the press.

95
00:06:56,910 --> 00:06:59,588
她也不需要编写产生那种熟悉的点击声的代码。
She didn't have to write the code that makes that familiar clicking noise.

96
00:07:00,014 --> 00:07:01,188
这些都已经为她准备好了。
It was already there for her.

97
00:07:01,742 --> 00:07:04,164
按钮实际上就是一个接口。
Buttons are in fact an interface.

98
00:07:04,622 --> 00:07:10,820
它们为开发者提供了控制交互的手段，同时屏蔽了具体的实现细节。
They provide the developer the means of controlling its interactions
while abstracting away how that actually works implementation-wise.

99
00:07:11,374 --> 00:07:19,968
这个按钮是 API，也就是应用程序编程接口的一部分，它由用于创建该应用的应用程序框架提供。
This button is part of the API, or application programming interface, and
it's available from the application framework that was used to create the app.

100
00:07:20,270 --> 00:07:27,236
因为大多数应用都需要按钮，所以这个接口已经被创建
并提供给我们的开发者，也就是应用程序程序员来使用。
Because most apps need buttons, this interface has been created and provided to our developer, or application programmer, to use.

101
00:07:27,726 --> 00:07:33,380
通过实现按钮接口，我们的开发者能够在按钮被点击时运行她的代码。
By implementing the button interface, our developer was able to make her code run when the button was clicked.

102
00:07:33,710 --> 00:07:42,148
值得注意的是，代码可能还使用了设备操作系统提供的媒体播放器 API，比如 Android 或 iOS。
It's worth noting that the code probably also uses a media player API that
is provided with the operating system of the device, like Android or iOS.

103
00:07:42,798 --> 00:07:48,868
这个媒体播放器 API 屏蔽了发送音频数据到硬件的细节。
This media player API abstracts away needing to understand details of how to send audio data to hardware.

104
00:07:49,390 --> 00:07:53,668
开发者只需要知道他们想调用媒体播放器 API 上的播放方法即可。
The developer knows they just want to call the play method on the media player API.

105
00:07:54,288 --> 00:07:59,172
我想，这首歌也不是存储在设备上的，而是通过网络流式传输的。
I suppose too, the song isn't stored on the device, it's being streamed over the network.

106
00:07:59,408 --> 00:08:03,492
所以，为了获取这些数据，已经通过互联网发起了一个基于 Web 的 API 调用。
So a web-based API call has been made over the internet to get that data.

107
00:08:03,950 --> 00:08:09,636
实际上，直到最底层的 0 和 1 都是由 API 提供的，它们在多个层级上提供了抽象。
It's actually APIs all the way down to the ones and zeros, providing abstractions at many levels.

108
00:08:10,286 --> 00:08:13,124
那么，让我们来看看所有这些接口的共同点是什么。
So let's take a look at what all of these interfaces have in common.

109
00:08:13,614 --> 00:08:20,368
它们都定义了我们与对象交互或通信的方式，无论那个对象是物理的还是软件的。
They all define ways for us to interact or communicate with an object, whether that object be physical or software.

110
00:08:20,686 --> 00:08:24,292
作为接口的使用者，我们不需要了解具体的实现。
And as a user of the interface, we don't need to understand the implementation.

111
00:08:24,528 --> 00:08:25,988
我们不需要知道它是如何工作的。
We don't need to know how it works.

112
00:08:26,368 --> 00:08:29,956
我们只需要知道被允许修改或看到的内容是什么。
We just need to know what we've been allowed to change or see.

113
00:08:30,382 --> 00:08:33,220
接口屏蔽了实现细节。
Interfaces abstract away the implementation.

114
00:08:33,774 --> 00:08:42,212
现在，虽然 UI 或用户界面是为应用程序用户设计的，但 API 是为应用程序程序员设计的，
供他们在应用程序中使用和扩展。
Now, while the UI or user interface is made for the user of the application, the
API is made for the application programmer to use and extend in their applications.

115
00:08:42,926 --> 00:08:49,316
既然我们已经了解了 API 中的 I，让我们深入了解一下 API 这个缩写中的其余部分。
With the I covered in our API, let's take a deeper look at the rest of the API acronym.

116
00:08:50,350 --> 00:08:52,260
API 就像是一种契约。
An API is a contract of sorts.

117
00:08:52,528 --> 00:08:57,892
它定义了预期的使用方式，以及通过使用它你可以期望得到什么。
It defines how it is expected to be used and it defines what you can expect to receive by using it.

118
00:08:58,542 --> 00:09:02,852
把它想象成一套为像你这样的软件开发者设计的工具集。
Think of it as a set of tools designed for software developers like yourself to use.

119
00:09:03,214 --> 00:09:10,148
API 的创建是为了让你的生活更轻松，因为它让你能够访问数据，并为你提供实现的抽象。
An API is created to help make your life easier by giving you access
to data and by providing you with an abstraction of the implementation.

120
00:09:10,638 --> 00:09:13,476
就是说，你不需要知道它具体是怎么运作的。
You know, not needing to know how it's doing whatever it's doing.

121
00:09:14,350 --> 00:09:17,264
它们能让你不用自己去创建所有东西。
They save you from needing to create everything yourself.

122
00:09:17,454 --> 00:09:21,744
就像设计得好的 API，只需几行代码就能实现几乎不可能完成的任务。
Like when designed well, they make nearly impossible things happen with just a few lines of code.

123
00:09:21,966 --> 00:09:25,316
现在，你可能已经开始明白为什么要熟悉它们了，对吧？
Now, you're probably starting to see why it's important to get familiar with them, right?

124
00:09:25,678 --> 00:09:30,784
我想先简单提醒一下，这里有一些你可能会遇到的命名问题。
I want to do a quick word of warning here about some naming issues that you'll probably run into.

125
00:09:31,024 --> 00:09:36,708
现如今，API 这个术语几乎总是被用来解释基于 Web 的 API。
These days, the term API is almost always used as a way to explain web-based APIs.

126
00:09:36,944 --> 00:09:43,424
事实上，你可以放心地假设，当你听到别人提到 API 时，他们很可能是在谈论基于 Web 的 API。
In fact, it's probably safe to assume that when you hear API in the
wild, it's most likely that people are talking about a web-based API.

127
00:09:43,598 --> 00:09:44,900
我们稍后会探讨这些内容。
We'll explore those here in a few.

128
00:09:45,646 --> 00:09:53,184
不过，如果我们关注所有的 API 类型，而不仅仅是基于 Web 的子集，
我们就能更好地了解为什么基于 Web 的 API 如此流行。
However, if we focus on all types of APIs, instead of just the web-based
subset, we'll get a better look into why web-based APIs are so popular.

129
00:09:53,358 --> 00:09:54,660
所以耐心点，我们马上就到那了。
So hang tight, we're getting there.

130
00:09:55,024 --> 00:09:59,024
在你的软件开发旅程中，API 无处不在，几乎无论你看到哪里都有。
APIs are everywhere in your software journey, just about everywhere you look.

131
00:09:59,310 --> 00:10:03,044
你的编程语言，无论是哪一种，都提供了许多 API。
Your programming language, whichever one that might be, provides many APIs.

132
00:10:03,278 --> 00:10:06,084
让我们来看看其中一个在所有语言中都很常见的 API。
Let's take a look at one that is common in all of them.

133
00:10:06,414 --> 00:10:10,624
它们都提供了一些处理字符串的方法，就是说，像一系列字符那样。
They all provide some way of working with strings, you know, like a series of characters.

134
00:10:11,022 --> 00:10:12,516
你想把你的文本全部变成大写吗？
Wanna make your text all uppercase?

135
00:10:12,782 --> 00:10:15,184
只需调用 API 方法，搞定。
Just call the API method, and voila.

136
00:10:15,726 --> 00:10:23,172
你可能可以通过对每个字符进行某种底层位运算来自己创建大写字符串，但你没必要这么做。
You could probably create the uppercase string all by yourself by doing
some sort of low-level bit math on each character, but you don't need to.

137
00:10:23,566 --> 00:10:28,388
因为你的编程语言已经为你把这些内容抽象掉了，并提供了一个好用的字符串 API。
Because your programming language has abstracted away that for you and provided a handy string API.

138
00:10:28,974 --> 00:10:32,420
它的存在是因为这是开发者经常遇到的常见问题。
It exists because this is a common enough problem that developers run into.

139
00:10:33,166 --> 00:10:42,852
因为这些编程语言规范仅仅是接口，不同的操作系统可以提供自己的实现，
以便代码能在它们的环境变量中正常运行。
Because these programming language specifications are just interfaces, different operating systems can supply their own implementation
so that the code works properly in their environment.

140
00:10:43,406 --> 00:10:49,956
我觉得一个简单的想象方式是，想想 Windows 或 Mac
OS 等不同操作系统是如何维护它们的文件系统的。
I think an easy way of picturing this is to think about how different operating systems like Windows or Mac OS maintain their files.

141
00:10:50,286 --> 00:10:51,524
它们完全不同。
They're totally different.

142
00:10:51,886 --> 00:10:55,876
然而，你的编程语言提供了一个统一的 API 来操作文件系统。
Yet your programming language provides a single API for working with the file system.

143
00:10:56,270 --> 00:11:00,624
你可以写一个脚本，它就能在 Mac 和 Windows 上同时运行。
You can write one script and it works both in Mac and Windows.

144
00:11:00,814 --> 00:11:03,664
是实现部分帮你处理了所有的细节。
It's the implementation that takes care of all of the details for you.

145
00:11:03,854 --> 00:11:04,676
挺酷的，对吧？
Pretty cool, right?

146
00:11:05,102 --> 00:11:06,784
这绝对是值得感激的事情。
Definitely something to be thankful for.

147
00:11:06,990 --> 00:11:11,428
不用担心正在使用哪个操作系统，这让我们的生活变得轻松多了。
It makes our lives so much easier not having to worry about what operating system is being used.

148
00:11:11,918 --> 00:11:15,424
而且，API 对一门语言的重要性是不可低估的。
And the importance of an API to a language cannot be understated.

149
00:11:15,758 --> 00:11:23,364
有一些非常有趣的法律案例，在这些案例中，人们质疑语言 API 本身——再次强调，
不是实现部分——是否可以受到版权保护。
There have been some fascinating legal cases where it's been questioned if
the language API itself, again, not the implementation, can be copyrighted.

150
00:11:23,598 --> 00:11:24,932
查看笔记了解更多相关信息。
Check out the notes for more on that.

151
00:11:25,344 --> 00:11:32,132
同样道理，还有不同的 Web 浏览器，比如 Google
Chrome、Microsoft Edge、Safari、Firefox 和 Brave。
Along similar lines, there are different web browsers, you know, like Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge, Safari, Firefox, Brave.

152
00:11:32,366 --> 00:11:38,820
现在，你可能以前没思考过，但你有没有觉得，能为 Web
编写代码且能在所有这些浏览器上运行，这难道不神奇吗？
Now, you might not have pondered this before, but isn't it amazing
that you can write code for the web and it will work in any of them?

153
00:11:39,118 --> 00:11:45,956
之所以能做到这一点，是因为有一套 Web API，所有的 Web
浏览器都必须实现这些 API，才能为你实现这些神奇效果。
This works because there's a set of web APIs that web browsers all must implement in order to make that magic happen for you.

154
00:11:46,478 --> 00:11:50,916
朋友们，我们是站在巨人的肩膀上，我们真的应该经常感谢那些巨人。
We're standing on the shoulders of giants, folks, and we really should thank those giants more often.

155
00:11:51,104 --> 00:11:57,284
有一些库的作者和维护者，他们把代码打包起来来解决常见问题，这样你就不用自己去做了。
There are authors and maintainers of libraries that package up code to solve common problems so that you don't need to do it yourself.

156
00:11:57,806 --> 00:12:01,700
例如，你知道如何通过编程方式把照片变成黑白的吗？
For instance, do you know how to programmatically make a photo black and white?

157
00:12:02,222 --> 00:12:02,784
我也不知道。
Me neither.

158
00:12:02,958 --> 00:12:07,664
但我可以把一个图像包下载并导入到我的应用中，突然我就会了。
But I can download and import an image package into my application, and suddenly I can.

159
00:12:07,854 --> 00:12:09,412
我只需要调用一个方法，砰的一声就搞定了。
I just call a method and boom.

160
00:12:10,222 --> 00:12:11,904
我真希望现实生活中的技能也能这样运作。
I wish real life skills worked this way.

161
00:12:12,110 --> 00:12:14,148
我想导入“跳舞”技能，这样在婚礼上就能更开心了。
I'd import dancing so it could be more fun at weddings.

162
00:12:14,638 --> 00:12:15,344
恰恰恰！
Cha-cha-cha!

163
00:12:15,982 --> 00:12:20,452
框架提供了一个 API，允许你扩展已有的功能，使其成为你自己的。
Frameworks provide an API that allow you to extend what has been provided to make it your own.

164
00:12:20,624 --> 00:12:25,988
比如，大多数 Web 框架都提供给你的能力，就是在你的 Web 应用中添加一个新的路由。
Like, for example, most web frameworks provide you with the ability to add a new route to your web application.

165
00:12:26,510 --> 00:12:29,796
它允许你将动态数据写入到你指定的模板中。
It allows you to write out dynamic data to a template that you specify.

166
00:12:30,254 --> 00:12:33,188
而你只需要用寥寥几行代码就能完成这一切。
And you do all of this with just a handful of lines of code.

167
00:12:33,646 --> 00:12:40,548
你不需要理解它是如何运作的，但你需要知道如何按照框架的预期去编写实现。
You don't need to understand how it's doing things, but you do need to
know how to write an implementation to what the framework is expecting.

168
00:12:40,878 --> 00:12:46,780
在这种情况下，你正在使用 API，也就是契约，来编写符合要求的有效实现。
In this case, you're using the API, the contract, to write a valid implementation that meets the requirement.

169
00:12:47,214 --> 00:12:50,532
现在，我们刚刚提到的所有这些 API，它们都是在我们的代码库本地运行的。
Now, all these APIs that we just talked about, they're local to our code base.

170
00:12:50,704 --> 00:12:52,064
它们存在于我们的代码中。
They exist within our code.

171
00:12:52,304 --> 00:12:54,564
我们使用它们是为了避免重复造轮子。
We use them to avoid recreating the wheel.

172
00:12:54,864 --> 00:13:03,940
也就是说，它们通过抽象掉大量工作来帮助我们完成需要执行的任务，
也就是那些我们开发者不应该去担心的事情。
That is to say, they assist us in accomplishing tasks that we need to perform by abstracting away a ton of the work for us,
the stuff that we developers shouldn't be worrying about.

173
00:13:04,174 --> 00:13:06,180
我们应该专注于眼前的业务问题。
We should be focusing on the business problem at hand.

174
00:13:06,446 --> 00:13:11,524
这意味着你的工作很大一部分就是弄清楚如何利用这些 API 来为自己谋利。
And that means a big part of your job is figuring out how to leverage these APIs to your advantage.

175
00:13:12,046 --> 00:13:15,504
现在，我觉得是时候让你休息一下了。
Now, I think this is an excellent time for you to take a break.

176
00:13:15,758 --> 00:13:19,556
我刚刚给了你很多思考的东西，我想让你花点时间喘口气。
I just gave you a lot to think about, and I'd like you to take some time and breathe.

177
00:13:20,622 --> 00:13:23,088
不如你记下一些你今天用过的 API？
Why don't you jot down some APIs that you've used today?

178
00:13:23,248 --> 00:13:26,128
即使你没在写代码，我知道你也看到了一些 API 在运行。
Even if you weren't coding, I know that you've seen some in action.

179
00:13:26,382 --> 00:13:30,308
如果你很难想到一些，我给你个提示。
If you're having a hard time thinking of some, I'll give you a hint.

180
00:13:30,894 --> 00:13:34,288
你现在正在 Web 浏览器里看一段视频。
You're watching a video right now inside of a web browser.

181
00:13:35,342 --> 00:13:44,128
回来之后，我们将探讨远程 API 的概念，即那些不在我们的代码库中本地执行的 API，
以及为什么这是一个如此强大的概念。
When we return, we'll explore the concept of remote APIs, ones that aren't
executing locally in our code base, and why that is such a powerful concept.

182
00:13:59,950 --> 00:14:01,888
我花了好一阵子才找到这个遥控器。
It took me a while to find this remote control.

183
00:14:02,318 --> 00:14:03,088
它就在沙发里。
It was in the couch.

184
00:14:03,438 --> 00:14:04,452
它总是就在沙发上。
It's always on the couch.

185
00:14:04,768 --> 00:14:07,568
不管怎样，这个遥控器上面有一个接口，对吧？
Anyways, this remote has an interface on it, right?

186
00:14:07,648 --> 00:14:08,548
这里有一个电源按钮。
There's a power button.

187
00:14:08,878 --> 00:14:11,328
当我按下频道按钮时，它就会切换频道。
And when I press the channel button, it changes the channel.

188
00:14:11,488 --> 00:14:19,396
当我按下音量按钮时，它会向我的收音机发送请求，音量就会改变，
就像我直接在设备本身的接口上调大音量一样。
When I press the volume button, it sends the request to my radio, and the volume
changes as if I turn the volume up using the interface on the device itself.

189
00:14:19,918 --> 00:14:21,348
我们知道为什么这很重要。
And we know why that's important.

190
00:14:21,646 --> 00:14:23,408
我想远程控制我的设备。
I want to remotely control my device.

191
00:14:23,568 --> 00:14:25,380
我不想从椅子上站起来。
I don't want to get up from my chair.

192
00:14:25,742 --> 00:14:30,212
当然，还有更多更懒惰的理由让我们想要远程控制对象。
There are, of course, more less lazy reasons as to why we'd want to remotely control objects.

193
00:14:31,118 --> 00:14:33,648
例如，这个可爱的小机器人有一个远程 API。
This cute little robot, for instance, has a remote API.

194
00:14:33,808 --> 00:14:37,956
你可以向它发送命令，它就会做出响应，甚至还会把传感器信息传回来。
You can send commands to it and it responds, and it even sends back sensor information.

195
00:14:38,286 --> 00:14:41,092
这些交通标志可以根据 API 调用而改变。
These traffic signs can change based on an API call.

196
00:14:41,678 --> 00:14:44,448
这里的无人机是由我手机上的一个 App 远程控制的。
This drone here is remotely controlled by an app on my phone.

197
00:14:44,688 --> 00:14:45,008
看到了吗？
See?

198
00:14:48,688 --> 00:14:53,220
而且受益于远程 API 的不仅仅是物理对象。
And it's not just physical objects that benefit from remote APIs.

199
00:14:53,518 --> 00:14:57,328
很多时候，我们的本地机器没有足够的空间来存储所有可用的数据。
Oftentimes we don't have space on our local machines for all the data that's available.

200
00:14:57,518 --> 00:15:01,860
比如，你有没有用过那种能帮你辨认正在播放什么歌曲的 App？
Like, for example, have you ever used one of those apps that helps you figure out what song is playing?

201
00:15:02,222 --> 00:15:08,644
你用麦克风录制一段你在超市听到的歌曲片段，它就会返回准确的歌曲名称。
You send up a snippet of a song that you heard playing at a grocery
store using your microphone, and it sends back exactly what song it is.

202
00:15:09,198 --> 00:15:10,244
通常是菲尔·科林斯。
It's usually Phil Collins.

203
00:15:10,368 --> 00:15:13,252
结果发现，超市和我都非常喜欢菲尔·科林斯。
It turns out that both grocery stores and yours truly love Phil Collins.

204
00:15:14,158 --> 00:15:19,780
你的手机里装不下世界上所有的歌曲，而有了这个远程 API，你就不需要拥有所有歌曲了。
You don't have room for all the songs in the world on your phone, and with this remote API, you don't need to have all of them.

205
00:15:20,174 --> 00:15:24,068
它负责所有的搜索和处理，而且速度快得惊人。
It does all the searching and processing, and it's surprisingly quick.

206
00:15:24,590 --> 00:15:26,308
它到底是怎么做到的？
How can it possibly do that?

207
00:15:26,926 --> 00:15:28,848
我不知道，但我很喜欢这个接口。
I don't know, but I love the interface.

208
00:15:28,928 --> 00:15:29,648
它太快了。
It's so fast.

209
00:15:29,808 --> 00:15:33,668
噢，这是远程 API 的另一个好处：计算能力。
Ooh, that's another benefit of remote APIs: computational power.

210
00:15:34,446 --> 00:15:40,768
因为 API 突破了你本地设备的限制，你可以获得看似无限的算力。
Because an API removes the limitations of your local device, you can gain access to seemingly infinite amounts of power.

211
00:15:41,008 --> 00:15:43,908
一个很好的例子就是谷歌翻译的 AR 应用。
A good example of this is the AR app of Google Translate.

212
00:15:44,558 --> 00:15:51,568
你可以使用手机摄像头，它几乎能实时地直接在屏幕上完成翻译，就像瞬间完成一样。
You can use the camera on your phone, and in near real time, it's able to translate directly to your screen, like instantaneously.

213
00:15:51,854 --> 00:15:57,860
它消耗了大量的处理能力来实现这种神奇效果，而这些处理是在别处，也就是在云端进行的。
It's using a ton of processing power to make that magic happen, and that processing is happening elsewhere, in La Nube.

214
00:15:58,766 --> 00:16:07,488
这经历了相当多的实验和想法的演变，但随着时间的推移，
我们终于把这个远程 API 的梦想变成了现实。
It took quite a bit of experimentation and some evolution of ideas, but over time,
we finally turned this remote API dream into a reality.

215
00:16:07,854 --> 00:16:15,888
现在我要把一些历史背景放入教师笔记中，但我只想让你们知道，
为了实现远程 API 这个概念，我们经历过非常真实的挣扎。
Now I'm gonna drop some history into the teacher's notes, but I just want you
to know that the struggle to achieve this concept of remote APIs was very real.

216
00:16:16,270 --> 00:16:19,968
现在，大部分问题在于当时还没有一个大家都认可的标准。
Now, most of the problem was that there wasn't a standard that everyone loved.

217
00:16:20,174 --> 00:16:23,076
在这些想法被摸索出来之前，情况一直挺笨重的。
Things were pretty clunky for a while as these ideas were sussed out.

218
00:16:23,568 --> 00:16:25,316
但后来发生了转折。
But then it happened.

219
00:16:25,648 --> 00:16:35,364
一种全新的架构风格出现了，它非常清晰，允许我们开发
者通过网络与特定的资源进行交互，它简直改变了一切。
A new architectural style appeared that was clear and allowed us developers to interact
with specific resources over the web, and it quite literally changed everything.

220
00:16:35,918 --> 00:16:42,628
这种风格迅速脱颖而出成为赢家，几乎所有人开始通过互联网提供和消费外部 API。
This style rapidly emerged as a winner, and just about everyone began offering and consuming external APIs over the internet.

221
00:16:43,278 --> 00:16:46,916
现在，我在这里谈论的是 REST，也就是表现层状态转移。
Now, what I'm talking about here is REST, or representational state transfer.

222
00:16:47,182 --> 00:16:51,160
这是我们需要的方案，而且说实话，这是我们开发者应得的抽象。
It was the approach that we needed, and honestly, it's the abstraction that we developers deserve.

223
00:16:51,662 --> 00:16:58,768
正如我们之前讨论过的，REST 的普及速度如此之快，以至于它几乎完全取代了 API 这个词。
Like we talked about already, the popularity of REST spread so rapidly that it nearly completely overtook the term API.

224
00:16:59,022 --> 00:17:05,028
就像我们用 Xerox 这个词来指代复印一样，尽管 Xerox 只是一个复印机品牌。
It's like how we use the word Xerox to mean photocopy, even though Xerox is just a brand of photocopier.

225
00:17:05,326 --> 00:17:08,367
我想 Xerox 还会做别的事情。
Xerox does other stuff too, I think.

226
00:17:09,230 --> 00:17:10,596
这就是 REST 的普及程度。
That's how pervasive REST is.

227
00:17:11,214 --> 00:17:14,688
REST 肯定不是远程 API 的终极标准。
REST is for sure not the end all be all of remote APIs.

228
00:17:14,848 --> 00:17:18,208
它肯定有一些局限性，我们稍后会讨论这些。
There are definitely some limitations, which we'll discuss here in a bit.

229
00:17:18,579 --> 00:17:24,194
技术在不断演进，当然还有一些运动仍在推动最佳远程 API 的概念。
Technology is ever evolving, and of course there's movements that are still advancing the concept of the best remote APIs.

230
00:17:24,622 --> 00:17:27,008
有些词你们可能甚至听过，比如 GraphQL。
Some terms you might even have heard, like GraphQL.

231
00:17:27,182 --> 00:17:28,452
更多内容请看教师笔记。
More on this in the teacher's notes.

232
00:17:29,038 --> 00:17:34,788
现在，在课程的剩余部分，我们将深入研究使用 REST 风格编写的 API。
Now for the rest of this course, we're going to do a deep dive into APIs that are written using the REST style.

233
00:17:35,246 --> 00:17:39,780
当 API 采用 REST 的风格和约束时，它们就被称为符合 REST 规范的。
When APIs embrace the style and constraints of REST, they are said to be RESTful.

234
00:17:40,526 --> 00:17:41,088
挺逗的，对吧？
Cute, right?

235
00:17:41,326 --> 00:17:47,168
如果你觉得需要休息一下，可以在我们深入探讨符合 REST 规范意味着什么之前先暂停视频。
If you feel like you need a little rest, go ahead and pause me before we do a deeper dive into what it means to be restful.

236
00:17:47,648 --> 00:17:52,688
抱歉，那个笑话很烂而且很明显，但作为开发者，你不得不开这种玩笑。
Sorry, that was a bad and obvious joke, but as a developer, you kind of have to make it.

237
00:17:52,768 --> 00:17:54,148
你真的没别的选择。
You don't really have an option.

238
00:17:55,278 --> 00:18:03,568
因为 REST 就像一顶非常漂亮的帽子一样建立在 Web 技术之上，
我们可能需要快速回顾一下 Web 是如何工作的。
Because REST sits on top of web technologies like a really nice hat, we should probably do a whirlwind refresher of how the web works.

239
00:18:18,768 --> 00:18:21,808
可能已经有一段时间没思考过 Web 是如何工作的了。
It might have been a while since you thought about how the web works.

240
00:18:21,968 --> 00:18:28,964
在进一步讨论 REST 如何使用这些术语之前，我想先让你们重新熟悉一下这些词汇。
There are a smattering of terms that I'd like to reload into your working memory before we talk more about how REST uses them.

241
00:18:29,328 --> 00:18:30,820
所以让我们来用一下网络。
So let's use the web.

242
00:18:31,438 --> 00:18:34,372
我的电脑就在这儿，上面装了个互联网浏览器。
I've got my computer here, and on it is an internet browser.

243
00:18:34,702 --> 00:18:39,428
浏览器是一个 Web 客户端，我将用它来连接到一台服务器。
The browser is a web client, and I'm going to use it to connect to a server.

244
00:18:39,886 --> 00:18:46,948
我将通过在地址栏中输入一个位置，准确地说，是一个通用资源定位符，也就是 URL。
I'm going to do this by putting a location in the address bar, a universal resource locator, or URL to be exact.

245
00:18:47,598 --> 00:18:53,284
你可能还会听到它被称为更广泛的术语 URI，即通用资源标识符。
You might also hear this referred to by its superset term, URI, universal resource identifier.

246
00:18:54,158 --> 00:18:59,652
现在我们的 URL 这里有一个协议部分，这个恰好是 HTTP。
Now our URL here has a scheme portion, and this one happens to be HTTP.

247
00:19:00,110 --> 00:19:03,268
HTTP 代表超文本传输协议。
HTTP stands for Hypertext Transfer Protocol.

248
00:19:03,598 --> 00:19:05,008
让我们稍微拆解一下。
Let's break that down a bit.

249
00:19:05,326 --> 00:19:07,088
我们来看看最后一部分，协议。
Let's grab that last part, protocol.

250
00:19:07,758 --> 00:19:10,608
协议通常也被比作一份契约。
A protocol is also often likened to a contract.

251
00:19:10,768 --> 00:19:13,636
它比 API 稍微低一点的层级，但概念上是相似的。
It's a little lower level than an API, but it's similar in concept.

252
00:19:14,126 --> 00:19:18,128
你可以把它理解为定义了沟通的预期。
You can think of it as defining the expectations of how to communicate.

253
00:19:18,288 --> 00:19:28,928
比如，一个让我印象深刻的例子是，如果你去过集会，有人做那种口号，比如我说 foo，
你就说 bar，foo，bar，就是那种。
Like, an example that I think really sticks with me is if you've ever been to a rally and someone does one of those chants where
they're like, when I say foo, you say bar, foo, bar, one of those.

254
00:19:29,102 --> 00:19:29,924
来，我们来做一个。
Here, let's do one.

255
00:19:30,190 --> 00:19:34,288
当我喊出 HTTP 时，你就喊出 P，HTTP，HTTP。
When I say HTT, you say P, H T T, H T T.

256
00:19:34,862 --> 00:19:38,288
噢，我刚刚意识到我可能让你大声喊出了 P。
Oh, I just realized I might've made you yell P out loud.

257
00:19:38,606 --> 00:19:39,748
希望你没在咖啡店里。
Hope you aren't in a coffee shop.

258
00:19:39,968 --> 00:19:40,580
好尴尬。
Awkward.

259
00:19:41,328 --> 00:19:45,008
总之，我们，也就是你和我，刚刚建立了一个快速的协议。
Anyways, we, you and I, just set up a real quick protocol.

260
00:19:45,198 --> 00:19:53,088
我告诉了你如何响应我的特定请求，如果你喊除了
P 以外的任何东西，你就是在破坏我们约定的协议。
I told you how to respond to my specific request, and you would have
been breaking our agreed-upon protocol if you yelled anything else but P.

261
00:19:53,774 --> 00:19:56,768
所以浏览器会为你创建一个 HTTP 请求。
So the browser creates an HTTP request for you.

262
00:19:56,974 --> 00:20:01,568
除了 URI，它还指定了一个特定的 HTTP 动词。
Along with the URI, it also specifies a particular HTTP verb.

263
00:20:01,774 --> 00:20:08,048
在这种情况下，它使用的是 GET 动词，用于明确这个请求只会接收数据。
In this case, it is using the GET verb, which is used to clarify that this request will only receive data.

264
00:20:08,208 --> 00:20:10,116
也就是说，它不会做出任何更改。
That is, it won't be making any changes.

265
00:20:10,448 --> 00:20:14,128
服务器接收到从客户端传输过来的请求。
The server receives the request that was transferred from the client.

266
00:20:14,288 --> 00:20:15,364
就是那个第二个 T。
There's that second T.

267
00:20:15,822 --> 00:20:18,048
然后服务器就会执行它需要执行的工作。
The server then does the work that it needs to do.

268
00:20:18,318 --> 00:20:22,308
最后，它生成结果网页并向客户端发送一个响应。
Finally, it generates the resulting web page and sends back a response to the client.

269
00:20:22,670 --> 00:20:23,844
在这种情况下，就是我的浏览器。
My browser in this case.

270
00:20:24,270 --> 00:20:26,928
响应中最重要的部分是正文。
The most important part of the response is the body.

271
00:20:27,118 --> 00:20:34,500
对于网页来说，它包含 HTML，也就是超文本标记语言，这对应了我们缩写中的 HT 部分。
For a web page, that contains the HTML, hypertext markup language, which gives us the remaining parts, the HT of our acronym.

272
00:20:34,766 --> 00:20:37,220
超文本就是可以链接到其他文本的文本。
Hypertext is text that can link to other text.

273
00:20:37,486 --> 00:20:40,132
在 HTML 中，我们通过锚点标签来实现这种链接。
In HTML, we perform that linking with anchor tags.

274
00:20:40,718 --> 00:20:43,488
所以拿到这个响应后，浏览器就会渲染出页面。
So with that response, the browser renders the page.

275
00:20:43,726 --> 00:20:50,768
当其中一个链接被点击时，它们会发起一个新的 HTTP GET 请求，这个过程会不断重复。
When one of those links are clicked, they kick off a brand new HTTP get request, and the process repeats itself over and over.

276
00:20:51,342 --> 00:20:53,968
不过在这些点击之间，协议的任务就完成了。
In between those clicks, though, the protocol's done.

277
00:20:54,158 --> 00:20:56,528
我们发出了请求，拿到了响应。
We've made the request, we got the response.

278
00:20:56,974 --> 00:21:00,420
HTTP 最初的设计是一个无状态协议。
HTTP as it was originally designed is a stateless protocol.

279
00:21:00,782 --> 00:21:03,748
一旦那个请求收到响应，一切就结束了。
Once that request receives a response, it's all done.

280
00:21:04,174 --> 00:21:10,212
如果你想保留某种状态，你的客户端必须自己管理这些状态，并在每次新请求时一起发送出去。
If you want to keep some sort of state, your client will have to manage that itself and send it up with each new request.

281
00:21:10,606 --> 00:21:12,068
查看笔记了解更多相关信息。
Check out the notes for more on this.

282
00:21:13,198 --> 00:21:17,328
除了 GET，还有更多的 HTTP 动词，也称为请求方法。
There are more HTTP verbs, also known as request methods, besides get.

283
00:21:17,808 --> 00:21:22,288
你最常看到的场景是遇到一个用于获取你信息的表单。
Most common scenario you see is when you encounter a form that is used to take information from you.

284
00:21:22,478 --> 00:21:28,928
通常你会看到使用 POST 动词，用来表示你正在向服务器提交或发送数据。
Typically you'll see the verb post used to explain that you are submitting or posting data to the server.

285
00:21:29,168 --> 00:21:36,004
好了，所以用来传输这些超文本的协议，坦白说比我们刚才的口号要复杂一点。
Okay, so our protocol that's used to transfer this hypertext is admittedly a little more complex than our rally chant was.

286
00:21:36,334 --> 00:21:41,028
请求和响应中包含了很多重要的信息。
There's quite a bit of important information in there that is included in the request and the response.

287
00:21:41,454 --> 00:21:45,764
你可能在 URL 中见过查询字符串参数，也就是问号后面的那些内容。
You've probably seen query string parameters in URLs and you know the stuff after the question mark.

288
00:21:46,048 --> 00:21:48,452
这是传递请求信息的一种方式。
This is one way to pass information about the request.

289
00:21:48,942 --> 00:21:54,276
另一种常见的传递信息的方式是通过特定的键值对，也就是请求头。
Another common way this information is passed around is through specific key and value pairs called headers.

290
00:21:54,528 --> 00:22:02,532
请求和响应都有请求头，它们用于帮助进一步沟通想要什么，以及返回了什么。
Both requests and responses have headers, and they're used to help
further communicate what is wanted, as well as what has been sent back.

291
00:22:02,928 --> 00:22:09,956
一些常用的请求头允许你指定你期望返回的内容信息，比如语言或内容类型。
Some of the popular request headers allow you to specify information about
the content that you expect back, like the language or the type of content.

292
00:22:10,382 --> 00:22:14,368
你还可以声明，如果你想要从某个时间点之后发生变化的页面。
You can also state that you want the page if it has changed since a certain time.

293
00:22:14,542 --> 00:22:17,728
这种技术支持缓存，这是一个非常强大的概念。
This technique allows for caching, which is a powerful concept.

294
00:22:17,838 --> 00:22:20,452
如果你已经在本地缓存里有了，为什么还要获取整个页面呢？
Why get the whole page if you already have it in your local cache?

295
00:22:21,326 --> 00:22:24,688
你可以使用请求头在每个请求中嵌入身份验证信息。
You can embed authentication information in each request using a header.

296
00:22:24,768 --> 00:22:28,260
通过这种方式，你基本上可以让你的客户端在多次请求之间保持登录状态。
And this way, you're essentially keeping your client logged in across requests.

297
00:22:28,878 --> 00:22:30,724
这个响应也包含请求头。
This response also contains headers.

298
00:22:30,928 --> 00:22:33,248
其中一个非常重要的就是状态码。
A super important one is the status code.

299
00:22:33,422 --> 00:22:37,924
这能让你知道服务端发生了什么，并传达一些信息，比如文档缺失。
This lets you know what happened on the server side and can convey things like missing documents.

300
00:22:38,318 --> 00:22:45,092
我敢肯定你见过 404 未找到，或者页面已移动，但希望一切都是 200 正常。
I'm sure you've seen the old 404 or maybe the page has moved, but hopefully everything is A200 okay.

301
00:22:45,678 --> 00:22:50,564
它还能解释内容类型、返回的数据有多大，以及更多信息。
It can also explain the content type, how large the data returned is, and many more things.

302
00:22:51,630 --> 00:22:52,912
好，明白了吗？
Okay, got it?

303
00:22:53,326 --> 00:22:55,908
刚才那场快速概览，你感觉怎么样？
Are you feeling good about that little whirlwind tour?

304
00:22:56,192 --> 00:22:59,472
记住，如果觉得内容太多，随时可以让我重复一遍。
Remember, feel free to make me repeat myself if that was too much.

305
00:22:59,632 --> 00:23:00,452
我完全理解。
I totally get it.

306
00:23:00,752 --> 00:23:02,596
刚才确实包含了很多信息。
That was a lot of information.

307
00:23:03,374 --> 00:23:07,152
对这些基础知识有基本了解很重要，因为 REST 是建立在这些基础之上的。
It's important to have a basic understanding of these things because REST sits on top of them.

308
00:23:07,246 --> 00:23:12,420
它利用了随着网络技术引入的概念，来暴露可以被访问和操作的资源。
It uses the concepts that were introduced with the web to expose resources that can be accessed and manipulated.

309
00:23:13,006 --> 00:23:19,620
让我们来看看符合 REST 规范的方法是如何利用网络及其工具来为您提供便利和获利的。
Let's take a look at how the RESTful approach takes advantage of the web and its tooling for your benefit and profit.

310
00:23:21,134 --> 00:23:24,352
REST 代表表现层状态转移。
REST stands for representational state transfer.

311
00:23:24,592 --> 00:23:29,552
符合 REST 架构风格约束的 API 被认为是符合 REST 规范的。
APIs that meet the REST architectural style constraints are considered to be RESTful.

312
00:23:29,902 --> 00:23:35,492
现在，一种典型的教学方式是先一次性讲完定义它的所有约束条件。
Now, a typical way to teach about RESTful APIs is to cover the constraints that define it, all up front.

313
00:23:35,822 --> 00:23:41,632
我不确定你是不是也一样，但就我个人而言，我觉
得这种学习方式压力很大，尤其是从一开始就学。
Now, I don't know about you, but personally I find approaching learning that way pretty overwhelming, especially from the beginning.

314
00:23:41,712 --> 00:23:43,712
我的意思是，我们甚至还没看过任何 API。
I mean, we haven't even looked at an API yet.

315
00:23:43,950 --> 00:23:48,644
再加上，你将要使用的大多数 REST API 都会符合所有这些约束。
On top of that, most of the REST APIs that you'll use will meet all of these constraints.

316
00:23:49,152 --> 00:23:50,592
所以情况是这样的。
So here's the thing.

317
00:23:50,862 --> 00:23:55,072
我承诺我们会过一遍所有的约束条件，但我们要在合适的时间再去做。
I promise that we'll go over all the constraints, but let's do it on our own time.

318
00:23:55,182 --> 00:23:57,284
也就是说，不要现在立刻把它们全部学完。
Like, don't learn them all right this instant.

319
00:23:57,742 --> 00:23:59,236
我们甚至可以把它变成一段有趣的旅程。
We could even make it a fun journey.

320
00:23:59,534 --> 00:24:02,340
就像一场符合 REST 规范的约束寻宝游戏。
Like a restful constraint scavenger hunt.

321
00:24:02,574 --> 00:24:03,812
还要有奖励！
Complete with prizes!

322
00:24:04,670 --> 00:24:05,572
禁止区域除外。
void where prohibited.

323
00:24:05,998 --> 00:24:11,460
以下是 API 被视为符合 REST 规范所需的指导性架构约束。
Here are the guiding architectural constraints required for an API to be considered RESTful.

324
00:24:11,726 --> 00:24:16,420
我们在讨论它们时会逐一勾选，然后在最后再循环一遍进行复习。
We'll check them off when we discuss them, and then we'll loop back once more at the end to review.

325
00:24:16,718 --> 00:24:17,668
必须全部收集齐。
Gotta collect them all.

326
00:24:18,126 --> 00:24:18,788
听起来有趣吗？
Sound fun?

327
00:24:19,406 --> 00:24:19,780
很好。
Good.

328
00:24:20,526 --> 00:24:26,148
让我们开始聊聊 REST API 是如何像戴小帽子一样建立在网络技术之上的。
Let's get started talking about how REST API sits on top of web technology, like a little hat.

329
00:24:26,542 --> 00:24:29,732
就像网络一样，客户端向服务器发送请求。
Much like the web, the client makes a request to a server.

330
00:24:30,094 --> 00:24:37,636
在这种情况下，客户端通常是你的程序，你可能会使用某种库来创建发送到服务器的请求。
The client in this case is typically going to be your program, and you'll
probably be using some sort of library to create the request to the server.

331
00:24:38,414 --> 00:24:43,076
使用的协议，你猜对了，就是 HTTP，而且它是无状态的。
The protocol used, you guessed it, it's HTTP, and it's stateless.

332
00:24:43,342 --> 00:24:46,404
服务器不会记住任何关于特定客户端的信息。
The server won't remember anything about the particular client.

333
00:24:46,606 --> 00:24:51,940
如果你想保持状态，比如你的登录详情，你必须在每一次请求中都发送它。
If you want to maintain state, like your login details, you must send it with each and every request.

334
00:24:52,206 --> 00:24:53,508
你会通过请求头来完成这个操作。
You'll do this using headers.

335
00:24:53,998 --> 00:24:59,332
就这样，我们讲完了 REST 的两个约束条件：客户端-服务器架构和无状态。
And just like that, we talked about two of our REST constraints, client-server architecture and stateless.

336
00:24:59,726 --> 00:25:01,392
你已经搞定两个了。
You got two knocked off already.

337
00:25:01,472 --> 00:25:02,564
你状态正佳。
You're on a roll.

338
00:25:03,406 --> 00:25:08,352
现在，我们应该谈谈那个请求到底在请求什么，对吧？
Now, we should probably talk about what that request was requesting, right?

339
00:25:08,686 --> 00:25:13,156
嗯，它肯定是在请求关于某个资源的信息。
Well, it most certainly was requesting information about a resource.

340
00:25:13,582 --> 00:25:16,912
现在，资源这个词有点抽象，这是故意的。
Now, a resource is a little abstract, and that's intentional.

341
00:25:17,134 --> 00:25:19,952
它是 URL 或 URI 中的 R。
It's the R in the URL or URI.

342
00:25:20,206 --> 00:25:23,364
我们使用资源这个术语来指代一个对象。
We use the term resource to reference an object.

343
00:25:24,398 --> 00:25:26,432
这恰好也很抽象，对吧？
Also happens to be pretty abstract, right?

344
00:25:26,702 --> 00:25:30,564
这是因为我们可以用资源来描述我们构建的几乎任何东西。
And this is because we can use resources to describe just about anything that we build.

345
00:25:30,766 --> 00:25:34,084
例如，让我们想象一个电子书商店网站。
For instance, let's imagine an ebook store website.

346
00:25:34,318 --> 00:25:37,828
在首页上，有一份新书的列表或集合。
On the homepage, there's a list or collection of new books.

347
00:25:38,254 --> 00:25:40,420
这些书中的每一本都是一个资源。
Each of these books is a resource.

348
00:25:40,750 --> 00:25:46,948
我们点击进去仔细查看那个书资源，我们看到了作者的链接，那也是一个资源。
We click in to take a closer look at that book resource and we see a link to the author, which is also a resource.

349
00:25:47,214 --> 00:25:51,332
我们点击进入作者资源，我们看到了他们写过的所有其他书的集合。
